#!perl

=head1 NAME

nergal - A mojolicious script for handling submissions of files for detonation.

=head1 SYNOPSIS

sudo -u cape nergal daemon -m production -l 'http://*:8080'

=head1 DESCRIPTION

This script is meant for running locally on a CAPEv2. It allows remote machines to
to submit files for detonation.

To work, this script needs to be running as the same user as CAPEv2.

This will write activity to syslog.

A systemd service file is provided at 'systemd/nergal.service' in this modules
tarball. It runs as the user and group 'cape' via the unit's 'User=' and 'Group='
directives, so edit those if you need a different user. It expects the enviromental
'/usr/local/etc/nergal.env' file to be setup with the variable 'LISTEN_ON'. So lets
say you want to listen on http://192.168.14.15:8080, it would be like below.

    LISTEN_ON="http://192.168.14.15:8080"

Alternatively, this script can be invoked as a CGI script if it is ran as the user CAPEv2 is.

=head1 CONFIGURATION

Everything is read from the cape_utils INI, '/usr/local/etc/cape_utils.ini' by
default, out of its root section. So if cape_utils has been configured and is
working, this just needs 'incoming' pointed somewhere usable.

The keys below are the ones specific to nergal. The rest of the INI is what it
always is, with how a submission is actually detonated coming from the usual
cape_utils settings, 'base', 'cape_runas', 'timeout', and the like.

    - incoming :: Directory incoming files are placed in for submission. The
      submission JSON, the checksum store, the name and task links, and the tmp
      dir a submission is staged in are all subdirectories beneath it. Must be
      writable by the user nergal runs as. See "INCOMING DIR STRUCTURE" below
      for the layout.
      - Default :: /malware/client-incoming

    - auth :: How a submitter is authed. 'ip' is by subnet only, 'apikey' by key
      only, 'both' requires the two of them, and 'either' takes one or the other.
      See "SECURITY" below.
      - Default :: ip

    - subnets :: Comma seperated list of subnets, v4 or v6, submissions are
      accepted from. Only used where 'auth' is 'ip', 'both', or 'either'.
      - Default :: 192.168.0.0/16,127.0.0.1/8,::1/128,172.16.0.0/12,10.0.0.0/8

    - apikey :: The key a submitter must send as the 'apikey' param. An empty
      value never matches, so key based auth is off until it is set.
      - Default :: ''

    - results_auth :: As 'auth', but for results fetching, GET /results/*, so
      that may be handed out seperately from the ability to submit. See
      "RESULTS FETCHING" below.
      - Default :: ip

    - results_subnets :: As 'subnets', but for results fetching.
      - Default :: 192.168.0.0/16,127.0.0.1/8,::1/128,172.16.0.0/12,10.0.0.0/8

    - results_apikey :: As 'apikey', but for results fetching.
      - Default :: ''

    - submission_gate :: Command ran per submission to decide what becomes of it,
      with the verdict taken from how it exits. Empty disables it. See
      "SUBMISSION GATE" below.
      - Default :: ''

    - submission_gate_timeout :: How many seconds the gate is given to run before
      it is killed and the submission dropped as an error.
      - Default :: 30

=head1 SECURITY

By default this will auth of the remote IP via the setting 'subnets', which by default is
'192.168.0.0/16,127.0.0.1/8,::1/128,172.16.0.0/12,10.0.0.0/8'. This value is a comma seperated
string of subnets to accept submissions from.

To enable the use of a API key, set the value of 'apikey' and set 'auth' to 'apikey'
(key only), 'both' (key and IP), or 'either' (key or IP).

=head1 SUBMISSION GATE

The 'submission_gate' setting is a command ran per submission to decide what
becomes of it. It is empty by default, which disables it. What is done with the
submission is taken from how it exits.

    - 0 :: Accept it. Saved and submitted as normal.

    - 1 :: Accept and save it, but do not submit it.

    - 2 :: Accept it, but neither save nor submit it. Nothing is kept.

    - 3 :: Deny it, temporarily. Replied to with a 444.

    - 4 :: Deny it, permanently. Replied to with a 445.

    - 5 :: Deny it, generically, for anything else. Replied to with a 403.

Anything else is a gate that is broken rather than one making a call, and is
handled like any other error, meaning the submission is dropped and replied to
with a 400. A gate that could not be ran, timed out, or was killed is treated the
same. So a broken gate never quietly accepts or denies anything, but a bad
'submission_gate' does stop submissions until it is fixed.

The gate is described the submission via 'NERGAL_' prefixed enviromental
variables, which include the file itself, the submitted JSON, the slug, the
checksums, the mime, and the flow info. Its stdout, stderr, and exit are all
written to syslog, and where the submission is saved they are recorded in the
incoming JSON as well. How long it is given to run is set via
'submission_gate_timeout', which defaults to 30 seconds.

See L<CAPE::Utils::Nergal/"SUBMISSION GATE"> for the full details.

=head1 SUBMISSION

Submissions must be made using the post method.

=head2 Submission Parameters

Required ones are as below.

    - filename :: The file being submitted.

The following are optional and more or less "free form",
but helps to set them to something sane and relevant.

    - type :: The type of submission. Generally going
              to be 'manual' or 'suricata_extract'.

    - json :: Metadata for the submission. For a submission
              coming from Suricata this is the EVE fileinfo
              record with a 'suricata_extract_submit' section
              added to it.

=head2 Submission Name Format

The 'json' param is optional. Where it is not sent, nergal will recover the flow
info and slug from the submitted filename provided it is in the standard format
below, which is what suricata_extract_submit builds its names in.

    <src_ip>-<src_port>-<dest_ip>-<dest_port>-<proto>-<sha1>-<slug>-<epoch>-<mime>

    - src_ip / dest_ip :: A valid IPv4 or IPv6 address.

    - src_port / dest_port :: An integer from 0 to 65535.

    - proto :: 'TCP' or 'UDP'. Case does not matter.

    - sha1 :: Hex digest of the file as of prior to sending, in upper, lower,
              or mixed case, and of any length. This is what the received file
              is checked against to detect a file changing in transit. Current
              submitters put a full sha1 here, but older ones put the first 18
              characters of the md5 instead and nothing in the name marks which
              it is, so it is recorded as '.suricata_extract_submit.md5' and
              nergal_extend compares it as a prefix against both the sha1 and
              the md5 taken on receipt.

    - slug :: The group/client/whathaveya the submitter belongs to. Must not be
              all digits. Stats are broken out per slug by nergal_extend.

    - epoch :: UTC unix time the file was extracted at, as a positive integer.

    - mime :: The mime type with any '/' replaced by '_', eg
              'application_x-dosexec'. Must therefore contain a '_'.

The mime is the only field that may contain a '-'. Nothing else in the name
may, the slug included, as the name is split on '-' with a limit of 9 and
everything past the ninth field is taken as part of the mime.

Matching is all or nothing. Unless all nine fields are present and valid the
name is left alone and nothing is filled in, so a manually submitted file keeps
its own name and simply has no flow info or slug. A slug containing a '-'
shifts every field after it and fails validation, so it is declined rather than
mis-parsed.

The name is only ever fallen back on where no usable 'json' was sent. A
submitter that sent a body is taken at its word about what the submission is and
has nothing filled in from the name, even the fields it left out. A body that
does not decode, or that decodes to something other than a hash, counts as none
being sent.

Only the file bit of the submitted name is ever used. A name containing path
separators is reduced to its last component, and one with nothing usable left
after that is rejected.

See L<CAPE::Utils::Nergal/parse_name>.

=head1 RESULTS FETCHING

Detonation results written by CAPEv2 under C<< <base>/storage/analyses/<task_id>/ >>
can be fetched via GET.

    GET /results/<task_id>            -- JSON array of available result files
    GET /results/<task_id>/<path>     -- fetch one result file

Only a fixed set of files may be fetched: C<reports/lite.json>,
C<reports/report.json>, C<reports/report.html>, C<reports/summary-report.html>,
and C<shots/*.jpg>. Anything else, including path traversal attempts, returns a
404.

This is gated separately from submission via the C<results_auth>,
C<results_apikey>, and C<results_subnets> config values. See
L<CAPE::Utils/check_remote_results>. The API key, if used, is passed as the
C<apikey> query parameter.

=head1 PINGING

If you submit a file of size 10 matching /1234567890/, so the ten bytes
"1234567890" and nothing else, it will return "TEST RECIEVED\n", provided the
submitter is authed. At that point it stops processing it, so nothing is saved
or submitted to CAPE.

=head1 ENVIROMENTAL VARIABLES

=head2 NERGAL_MAX_REQ_SIZE

This is the max request size, which will affect the size of files the can be submitted.

If undef, this defaults to 1G, 1073741824.

=head1 INCOMING DIR STRUCTURE

The following directories are used under the incoming directory.

    sha256
    json
    name_to_sha256
    task_to_json
    tmp

Any of them that are missing are created at start up, so only the incoming
directory itself has to be made beforehand. That one is never created for you,
as it is normally a mount and creating it would turn a failed mount into samples
landing on the wrong filesystem. Where it is missing, that is logged and every
submission is refused until it turns up, at which point nergal picks up again
without needing a restart.

"tmp" is where the file is written to first to get the checksum.

"sha256" is where it is moved to after we have the checksum if it does not
already exist.

"json" is where the incoming JSON is written to based on filename.

"name_to_sha256" contains links from incoming filename to the sha256
in question. If it already exists it is updated to the newest one.

"task_to_json" contains links to from the task name to the JSON it is for.

=cut

use Mojolicious::Lite -signatures;
use CAPE::Utils::Nergal  ();
use CAPE::Utils::LogDrek qw( log_drek );

# one instance shared across requests so the tracking int keeps incrementing
my $submitter = CAPE::Utils::Nergal->new;

# Make the incoming sub dirs up front so a fresh install works without them being
# created by hand, which otherwise 400s every submission until someone notices.
#
# Not fatal, as the incoming dir may be a mount that is not up yet and receive
# checks per submission anyway, so this recovers on its own once it appears. It
# is logged to both syslog and STDERR so it still shows in the journal.
eval { $submitter->make_dirs; };
if ($@) {
	my $error_message = 'incoming dir setup failed, submissions will be refused until it is sorted... ' . $@;
	log_drek( 'err', $error_message, undef, 'nergal' );
	warn($error_message);
}

# results fetching. list what result files exist for a task...
get '/results/:task_id' => sub ($c) {
	my $result = $submitter->results_list(
		task_id   => $c->stash('task_id'),
		remote_ip => $c->tx->original_remote_address,
		apikey    => $c->param('apikey'),
	);
	$c->render( text => $result->{body}, status => $result->{status} );
	return;
};

# ...and fetch a single one of them. '*subpath' captures the slashes in eg
# 'reports/lite.json' or 'shots/0001.jpg'. 'path' can't be used as the
# placeholder name as it is a reserved Mojolicious stash value.
get '/results/:task_id/*subpath' => sub ($c) {
	my $result;
	eval {
		$result = $submitter->results_fetch(
			task_id   => $c->stash('task_id'),
			path      => $c->stash('subpath'),
			remote_ip => $c->tx->original_remote_address,
			apikey    => $c->param('apikey'),
		);
	};
	if ($@) {
		log_drek( 'err', $@ );
		$c->render( text => "Error... please see syslog\n", status => 400 );
		return;
	}

	if ( $result->{path} ) {
		$c->res->headers->content_type( $result->{content_type} );
		$c->reply->file( $result->{path} );
	} else {
		$c->render( text => $result->{body}, status => $result->{status} );
	}
	return;
}; ## end sub

# two are needed as /* and / don't overlap... so pass it to the_stuff to actually handle it
post '/*' => sub ($c) {
	the_stuff($c);
};
post '/' => sub ($c) {
	the_stuff($c);
};

# Handles a submission, adapting between Mojolicious and the backend by pulling
# the pieces of the request off the controller, handing them to
# CAPE::Utils::Nergal->receive, and rendering whatever comes back.
#
# It exists because the two routes it backs, '/*' and '/', do not overlap in
# Mojolicious and so both have to be declared, and neither wants its own copy of
# this.
#
# The failure detail goes to syslog rather than into the response, as a submitter
# that has just tripped over something has no need to be told the internals of
# it.
#
# Args:
#     - c :: The Mojolicious::Controller for the request. The remote address,
#       the apikey and json parameters, the uploaded file, and whether the
#       request ran past the max request size are taken off it. Required.
#
# Returns nothing, the point of it being the response it renders. That is what
# receive() decided on, or a 400 when receive() died.
#
#     post '/*' => sub ($c) {
#         the_stuff($c);
#     };
sub the_stuff {
	my $c = $_[0];

	my $result;
	eval {
		$result = $submitter->receive(
			remote_ip => $c->{tx}{original_remote_address},
			apikey    => $c->param('apikey'),
			raw_json  => $c->param('json'),
			upload    => $c->param('filename'),
			oversized => $c->req->is_limit_exceeded,
		);
	};
	if ($@) {
		log_drek( 'err', $@ );
		$c->render( text => "Error... please see syslog\n", status => 400, );
		return;
	}

	$c->render( text => $result->{body}, status => $result->{status}, );

	return;
} ## end sub the_stuff

# ensure we have something set for $ENV{'NERGAL_MAX_REQ_SIZE'} and that it is a
# int
if ( defined( $ENV{'NERGAL_MAX_REQ_SIZE'} ) ) {
	if ( $ENV{'NERGAL_MAX_REQ_SIZE'} !~ /^\d\d*$/ ) {
		log_drek(
			'err',
			'$ENV{NERGAL_MAX_REQ_SIZE} set to "'
				. $ENV{'NERGAL_MAX_REQ_SIZE'}
				. '" which does not match /^\d\d*$/ ... defaulting to 1073741824',
		);
		$ENV{'NERGAL_MAX_REQ_SIZE'} = 1073741824;
	}
} else {
	$ENV{'NERGAL_MAX_REQ_SIZE'} = 1073741824;
}
app->max_request_size( $ENV{'NERGAL_MAX_REQ_SIZE'} );

app->start;
